 [17592186054722 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054722 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054722 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054722 #edb/ref 1039 "Sketching the Graph of a Parabola Given Its Equation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054722 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186054488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054722 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "867af724-0aa0-599a-8a5f-ee753fd19414" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054926 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054926 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054926 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054926 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054926 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054926 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054926 #edb/ref 1045 "Graphs of General Quadratic Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054926 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186055713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054926 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054926 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054926 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e7a68f82-01c3-5f8e-ac40-f72fcee7a2b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055158 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186052277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055158 #edb/ref 1069 "q-24393" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055158 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055158 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f5/f57f1223c28fc554909be6bbd2e8eafefa21a886b1a5abb47549f17bfa12e2ab.png)\n\nFor the parabola shown above, find the vertex and the equation of the axis of symmetry." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055158 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e6ba3114-4b55-54d8-a439-a9e561cd3410" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055158 #edb/ref 1074 "The vertex is the \"turning point\" of the parabola. In an upwards parabola, like the one here, the vertex is the minimum point. So, the vertex of this parabola is $(3,-2).$\n\nThe axis of symmetry is the vertical line that passes through the vertex. Therefore, the axis of symmetry of this parabola is $x=3.$\n\n![](images/f1/f139192260875d5e988f30a64a7024f50d68337dbd4969d938f18aa62b850dd0.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055652 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055652 #edb/ref 1061 27352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055652 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "128181ff-b771-504f-87d8-8018de0e4105" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055652 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055713 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055713 #edb/ref 1061 4546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055713 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d642e4f2-9830-5f74-a1bf-20c4e26c8ac1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055713 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186055952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055714 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055714 #edb/ref 1061 4547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055714 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f933d693-32d6-57d7-a9d6-8e75f0fb5999" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055714 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055715 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055715 #edb/ref 1061 4550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055715 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4eea49a1-27f3-5ec1-8c5c-c52dee47ff51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055715 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055716 #edb/ref 1061 4553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055716 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ef88d516-7d9b-56b6-8c46-e0b80b4f034e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055716 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055952 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the graph of the parabola ![](images/f2/f218c572691a74963cd3808bf4df2923122e84b65d1fabf1138bab6264b043b5.svg) shown below.\n\n![](images/45/450251899f728a3cc927b72fdb7655204828c8e0be0432cb08cee2462d6e5bfe.png)\n\nWe can describe many features of the graph using mathematical terminology:\n\n- The ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-intercepts of the parabola are at ![](images/81/8171bb8403bd612f56ef5f32f400f1aef8efeed51ea279d36779771fdea1ae0d.svg) and ![](images/77/779cec712506e0f798805963ab85841b46650fb22681d97a64aec4b85a47d07f.svg) We say that ![](images/b0/b064281df4bef2506521322c7477468b9cc565616e2c5b6f21000df6522399c1.svg) and ![](images/1f/1fb9f2f73a09599931892c1dee7629975b4d851e5dd6f0d07b7c641bbf17bf54.svg) are the **roots** (or **zeros**) of the parabola.\n\n- The parabola intersects the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis at  ![](images/6a/6a0e2cbf9f56c3c989ad96f020fe8bb0f7c785ff6de8073c476232b95476ac13.svg). We say that ![](images/78/78d4465d85b55cee3832a9b598a2564b6a3fa9a072de8a1fb4b40ca884079670.svg) is the **![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept**. The ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept is the same as the constant term of the function.\n\n- The parabola has a \"turning point\" at  ![](images/ef/ef43561f9d825c9e79fb03dffafa744d386d0c2a5a07da903675bca120d59fd1.svg) which is called the **vertex**. In an upwards parabola (like the one shown above), the vertex is the minimum point, while in a downwards parabola, the vertex is the maximum point.\n\n- The vertical line  ![](images/df/df9822243c11ec9f0fabca5cc578976d0bdea2c521b4f61964f5f3850a970504.svg) that passes through the vertex is called the **axis of symmetry**. The part of the parabola to the left of the axis of symmetry is the mirror image of the right, and vice versa.\n\n- This particular parabola is **upward opening** because it has a distinctive ![](images/13/13ab0b72e7963f3e74404b8b75f12654ece94c2c44e1fb09fdf230b976eb681b.svg) shape. A parabola ![](images/7c/7c49eede8f0eb80dadd8384ae942dc8248ef454eeb6afa1aeba7ae48e7545456.svg) is upward opening if the quadratic coefficient is positive ![](images/d2/d2a0fc4d90b45e06999c89b1a40ff6f01116e9c429c195adbfced573f9e47660.svg)\n\n- Parabolas can also be **downward opening** with a distinctive ![](images/57/57872695daf3067460adec5a506cfb1457729de4eeac879eab9f7355570bac11.svg) shape. A parabola ![](images/7c/7c49eede8f0eb80dadd8384ae942dc8248ef454eeb6afa1aeba7ae48e7545456.svg) is downward opening if the quadratic coefficient is negative ![](images/a1/a16d199f292dbd74941402b3d485cf107a7a985d28aeb13ab50ff6baf87f2d4b.svg)\n\nLet's take a look at an example of a downward opening parabola." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055952 #edb/ref 1065 1643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055952 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "38157cb0-baf6-5463-917a-3e22397a6104" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055952 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233089 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233089 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106046" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233089 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233089 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/cd/cd847efd8b91b1c622e93b1ee97035bcee803a79a603aa7b4beee8fae77abf06.png)\n\nThe equation of the curve above is $y=-{x}^{2}+2x+k.$ Find the value of $k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233089 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4cc27684-d306-5b1a-a65f-84d96866a22e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233089 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that if we substitute $x=0$ into the given equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{x}^{2}-x+k \\\\ y & =-(0)^{2}+2(0)+k \\\\ y & =k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nmeaning that the curve intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,k).$ Based on the graph, we see that we must have $k=5$ since our parabola intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234449 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234449 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83561" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234449 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234449 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ca/ca545b153cd04a539f30030c27a90dc4489b3a620e9a0d859bae5b531fcc7144.png)\n\nThe equation of the curve above is $y=\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-3x+k.$ Find the value of $k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234449 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fffc2429-ebec-5416-9ec6-594433fbf3de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234449 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that if we substitute $x=0$ into the given equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-3x+k \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{2}(0)^{2}-3(0)+k \\\\ y & =k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nmeaning that the curve intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,k).$ Based on the graph, we see that we must have $k=\\frac{1}{2}$ since our parabola intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528323 #edb/ref 1080 "images/c9/c956047bf140923ce38416d6f70374c94c37ae1c067584ced3ba4c831f036360.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528323 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528323 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "08f8b5c5-5a79-5789-bffa-95fbf9c0cca6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528326 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528326 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528326 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ac7d6e1-050b-59bb-8459-6b9fce755995" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528335 #edb/ref 1080 "$x=3$ and $x=6$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528335 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528335 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0fb0d9a4-9047-58f4-8e38-7b273aec2f8b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528336 #edb/ref 1080 "images/7f/7f2bd4a766b0acd0ddfa4c613b8b145cfc1065c2bd68bd4225262c998cd85f3a.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528336 #edb/re